00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:56.650
One million Graco and Simplicity cribs were recalled.

257
00:11:56.890 --> 00:12:03.990
And I even read an article that called 2007, quote, the year of the recall.

258
00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:05.970
Well, that's crazy.

259
00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:07.810
I wonder if it was in the zeitgeiSt.

260
00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:10.470
It was in the group mind of the world.

261
00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:15.110
And it went into Lee's brain and he shouted it into the writer's room.

262
00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:15.690
Yeah.

263
00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:17.410
And it became an episode of The Office.

264
00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:18.870
Major group mind.

265
00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:20.370
That's some global group mind.

266
00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:21.290
Okay, lady.

267
00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:23.450
I'm going to move us along to fast fact number three.

268
00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:25.810
I'm calling it location alert.

269
00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:27.130
Okay.

270
00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:33.070
As part of this episode, Jim and Andy go to a local high school to apologize for the obscene

271
00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:33.830
watermark.

272
00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:37.710
And Kentopedia let us know because we got some mail.

273
00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:40.950
People wanted to know, was this the same school where we shot Diwali?

274
00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:43.370
People love to know about locations.

275
00:12:43.750 --> 00:12:44.690
They love it.

276
00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:48.990
This was filmed at a different school than Diwali.

277
00:12:49.190 --> 00:12:52.930
This time we filmed at St. John Paul Academy in Burbank.

278
00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:57.810
In real life, it is a private Catholic STEM immersion high school.

279
00:12:58.030 --> 00:13:01.070
The school colors are white, blue, and red.

280
00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:03.390
And the real students at the school wear uniforMs.

281
00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:07.710
For our shoot, we hired background performers to play the students.

282
00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:11.150
We did not use the actual students of this school.

283
00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:16.350
I feel like that is a thorough answer to this location question.

284
00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:17.150
Yeah.

285
00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:19.570
You know, I went to a Catholic high school.

286
00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:20.490
I know this.

287
00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:21.530
It was not co-ed.

288
00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:23.350
It was all girls.

289
00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:24.910
And I wore a uniform.

290
00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:29.430
And our colors were green and white and gray and navy.

291
00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:31.770
I think were the colors we were allowed to wear.

292
00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:32.270
And yellow.

293
00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:33.850
That's a lot of colors.

294
00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:36.390
It's really only five.

295
00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:39.490
And you're only allowed to wear those colors.

296
00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:40.490
Seems like a lot.

297
00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:41.690
Scale it back.

298
00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:43.750
Do you wear...

299
00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:46.870
How many colors do you wear?

300
00:13:47.090 --> 00:13:50.450
I just grab whatever's, you know, there.

301
00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:51.850
Right.

302
00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:56.110
So imagine you're getting dressed in the morning and you have to limit it to only these five

303
00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:56.510
colors.

304
00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:57.310
Well, listen.

305
00:13:57.490 --> 00:13:59.690
A friend of mine did that thing.

306
00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:02.150
Remember when ladies would get their colors done?

307
00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:03.390
Oh, yes.

308
00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:05.290
And you find out if you're like fall or spring.

309
00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:07.250
A summer or a winter or something.

310
00:14:07.370 --> 00:14:08.170
My mom did this.

311
00:14:08.270 --> 00:14:09.330
My mom was a summer.

312
00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:10.170
Okay.

313
00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:13.090
So a friend of mine recently did this.

314
00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:13.910
It's sort of...

315
00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:14.910
Wait, they're still doing this?

316
00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:15.890
They're still doing it.

317
00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:24.250
That she's like shades of blue and coral and white.

318
00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:27.530
And she just filled her closet with those colors.

319
00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:29.010
She's always in those three colors.

320
00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:30.770
So what...

321
00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:31.550
Do you know what she is?

322
00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:32.450
Is that summer?

323
00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:33.470
I don't know.

324
00:14:33.630 --> 00:14:34.370
Coral, blue, and white?

325
00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:35.570
I don't know what it is.

326
00:14:35.890 --> 00:14:36.730
Maybe spring.

327
00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:40.050
But listen, it's a commitment to stay in those three colors.

328
00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:41.390
I'd have a hard time.

329
00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:42.830
Oh, my gosh.

330
00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:44.810
This is literally what I was saying.

331
00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:47.310
I said I could only wear five colors.

332
00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:48.930
And you're like, it's a lot of colors.

333
00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:50.290
I don't see what the issue is.

334
00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:53.310
And now you're like, I would have a hard time only wearing a few colors.

335
00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:58.670
My point was, if you're going to have a school that has uniforms, you can't have a

336
00:14:58.670 --> 00:14:59.490
lot of options.

337
00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:01.130
Why?

338
00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:02.810
Because you're already...

339
00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:05.610
You can't have personal expression within uniform?

340
00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:06.390
No.

341
00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:07.790
Oh, my gosh.

342
00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:08.770
You're taking a stand.

343
00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:14.030
You've clearly not thought about this at all because you are taking multiple positions

344
00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:15.470
on this one subject.

345
00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:20.710
What's the point of having a uniform if you can have multiple colors and freedom of expression?

346
00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:21.970
There's no point.

347
00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:24.350
Either have a uniform or don't.

348
00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:25.050
All right.

349
00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:27.450
Well, we had to all wear the same skirt.

350
00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:28.370
It was the same.

351
00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:29.450
There was no choice.

352
00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:31.430
Or navy pants.

353
00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:34.650
Or a yellow shirt or a green shirt or a white shirt.

354
00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:37.470
We have to be done talking about this.

355
00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:40.050
On top, you could wear the different colors.

356
00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:44.570
But they had to be a collared shirt, and you couldn't have any logos.

357
00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:45.110
No logos.

358
00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:46.130
No logos.

359
00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:53.070
Not even like, you know, some people, they would try to sneak in like a little polo on

360
00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:55.330
their lapel, and that would be a no.

361
00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:56.350
Wow.

362
00:15:57.370 --> 00:15:57.650
Because that...

363
00:15:57.650 --> 00:15:58.150
Lock it up.

364
00:15:58.330 --> 00:15:59.650
Lock it up, uniform.

365
00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:00.530
Lock it up.

366
00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:02.330
Lady, where are we?

367
00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:05.890
Well, we're ready to take a break, and then we're going to come back and break down this

368
00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:06.310
episode.

369
00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:11.030
Well, before we take a break, I want to just share one thing about this episode.

370
00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:14.790
Well, I'll just get to it.

371
00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:16.450
What is it?

372
00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:24.550
I had the hugest zit on my cheek, and I remember being really bummed out because I had a nice

373
00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:29.810
big storyline, and I woke up with an enormous zit on my cheek, and they tried to cover it

374
00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:30.210
with makeup.

375
00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:33.290
Jenna, they didn't splurge for CGI and scoochy it out.

376
00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:35.070
It's in the whole episode.

377
00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:39.050
There's so much makeup on it, it's like an orange patch on my cheek.

378
00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:43.350
I did not notice it one time in watching this.

379
00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:45.910
Do you have time codes for it?

380
00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:48.090
Is that something we can look forward to after the break?

381
00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:48.530
Yes.

382
00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:49.450
Yes.

383
00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:50.650
Well, there you go, guys.

384
00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:51.350
If you want a close up.

385
00:16:51.530 --> 00:16:53.370
Well, listen, I'm sure you're riveted now.

386
00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:54.810
You can't wait to come back.

387
00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:11.160
This episode with a very funny cold open.

388
00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:15.960
I think it is one of the classics of all the cold opens on the whole series.

389
00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:16.940
A hundred percent.

390
00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:25.900
It is Jim, dressed as Dwight, acting like Dwight, and it makes Dwight crazy in some of the best

391
00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:27.380
lines right here in this cold open.

392
00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:33.900
Lady, I remember filming this episode, and John Krasinski was so funny, we could not hold

393
00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:34.360
it together.

394
00:17:34.360 --> 00:17:39.420
Well, we all knew how great he was at impressions, and so when he got to actually flex that a

395
00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:42.060
little, we were just like, ah, I can't wait to watch.

396
00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:48.240
Well, Angela, I think this cold open has two of our most quoted moments.

397
00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:49.360
For sure.

398
00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:53.780
Number one, Bears beats Battlestar Galactica.

399
00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:55.900
I mean, how many t-shirts is that on?

400
00:17:56.020 --> 00:17:56.640
It's on everything.

401
00:17:56.980 --> 00:18:00.560
And also, identity theft is not a joke, Jim.

402
00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:03.200
Millions of families suffer every year.

403
00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:04.280
That's right.

404
00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:06.220
I see that all the time.

405
00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:08.420
Identity theft is not a joke.

406
00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:12.080
Don't we sell pencils that say, identity theft is not a joke?

407
00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:14.720
Don't we have a pencil pack on officeladies.com?

408
00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:15.460
Yes.

409
00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:17.460
And we have that quote, right?

410
00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:18.240
We have that quote.

411
00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:20.480
And we also have Bears beats Battlestar Galactica.

412
00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:22.760
It's just one of my favorite quotes.

413
00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:25.280
It is not my favorite quote of the whole episode, though.

414
00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:26.180
We'll get to that.

415
00:18:26.180 --> 00:18:28.760
But this couplet is so fun.

416
00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:33.000
And I have seen people, like, at conventions and stuff say this.

417
00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:33.400
False.

418
00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:34.060
Black bear.

419
00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:35.200
Like, the whole thing.

420
00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:36.260
Oh, yeah.

421
00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:37.400
Let's, we should play it.

422
00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:37.720
Sam.

423
00:18:37.780 --> 00:18:38.680
Yeah, let's play it.

424
00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:39.540
Play this clip.

425
00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:40.680
Question.

426
00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:43.360
What kind of bear is best?

427
00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:45.860
That's a ridiculous question.

428
00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:46.360
False.

429
00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:47.340
Black bear.

430
00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:48.640
That's debatable.

431
00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:50.880
There are basically two schools of thought.

432
00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:51.260
Fact.

433
00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:52.980
Bears eat beets.

434
00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:53.160
Oh.

435
00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:57.060
Bears beats Battlestar Galactica.

436
00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:57.780
Bears do not.

437
00:18:58.420 --> 00:18:59.860
What is going on?

438
00:19:00.020 --> 00:19:01.160
What are you doing?

439
00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:03.900
I love it so much.

440
00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:07.260
I want to know, what are the two schools of thoughts about Bears?

441
00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:09.040
We never find out.

442
00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:09.800
We don't.

443
00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:17.440
Listen, we had a fan question from Ashley Trick, Emily Dennis, Faith L, Rianne Holib, and Tara Laporte.

444
00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:20.940
Basically, they all said the same version of this.

445
00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:24.740
Please, please, please tell us everything about the cold open.

446
00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:27.880
It is one of the best sequences on television ever.

447
00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:30.820
How long did it take John and Rain to get through it?

448
00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:32.900
Here's what I found out, Angela.

449
00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:33.560
Okay.

450
00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:38.040
Kentopedia thinks we only did about eight takes of this.

451
00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:42.140
He said it went a lot faster than we may realize.

452
00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:44.160
Well, it was a quick beat.

453
00:19:44.420 --> 00:19:45.740
You know, it was a quick scene.

454
00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:52.840
And then I asked Justin, and he said that he and Brent did not write this cold open.

455
00:19:53.420 --> 00:19:58.020
They wrote a cold open where Michael is getting scammed by a telemarketer.

456
00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:07.800
But during the rewriting process, Gene and Lee are the ones who came up with the idea for Jim to come in as Dwight, and they wrote the cold open.

457
00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:13.860
And it was Mike Shore who pitched the line, Bears beats Battlestar Galactica.

458
00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:17.300
Applause, applause.

459
00:20:19.300 --> 00:20:21.720
This is why you need a whole writing team.

460
00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:23.400
Look at that.

461
00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:25.640
That's how the magic happens.

462
00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:27.740
It's a collaboration, people.

463
00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:31.240
I have two other observations about this cold open.

464
00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:33.400
Not as popular as the obvious ones.

465
00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:35.480
Bears beats Battlestar Galactica, all that.

466
00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:40.580
But I have you know, there is a new plant at reception.

467
00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:41.400
No.

468
00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:42.820
Get out of here.

469
00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:44.840
What is going on?

470
00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:53.260
I am telling you, I don't know when I'll have time to do this, but I am going to screen grab every plant on reception for the whole series.

471
00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:54.800
What is it this week?

472
00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:57.080
It's a real sad looking plant.

473
00:20:57.080 --> 00:20:58.880
It's only got a few leaves on it.

474
00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:01.920
It's in a purple pot with, it looks like some polka dots.

475
00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:03.860
And it is through the whole episode.

476
00:21:04.060 --> 00:21:06.600
I noticed it's in the cold open, the tag, and the whole episode.

477
00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:13.960
And in Jim's Talking Head, where he says he created that whole look for $11, we have an old tech alert.

478
00:21:15.260 --> 00:21:18.580
Old tech alert, we have the old calculator watch.

479
00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:20.020
Oh, yeah.

480
00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:27.620
Now, I know that Dwight always wears one, but just to see Jim actually use it, like you hear the beeping as he's adding on it.

481
00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:28.820
I just thought it was really fun.

482
00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:31.520
How often do you use the calculator on your phone?

483
00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:32.740
Surprisingly a lot.

484
00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:33.540
Same.

485
00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:34.360
Same.

486
00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:36.360
I see why they made a calculator watch.

487
00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:36.820
Yeah.

488
00:21:36.940 --> 00:21:39.280
Because, you know, I use it.

489
00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:47.160
I mean, I remember, like, because I'm 100 years old, that, you know, boys would have it at school, and they would be told in math class they could not use it.

490
00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:53.940
You know, I spent a year where I refused to let myself use a calculator, and I had to do all of my math problems by hand.

491
00:21:53.940 --> 00:22:01.180
And it was just a weird commitment I made to not, you know, I don't know.

492
00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:11.310
I mean, because I felt sort of like I should be able to add 26 and 97 without a calculator.

493
00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:14.730
Like, just, you know, carry the one or whatever.

494
00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:16.950
How old were you when you made this commitment?

495
00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:18.110
I was in my 30s.

496
00:22:18.310 --> 00:22:18.670
Really?

497
00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:19.490
Yeah.

498
00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:23.490
I was really expecting you to say I was 24.

499
00:22:25.070 --> 00:22:25.610
No.

500
00:22:25.870 --> 00:22:31.490
I just got disappointed in myself for relying on my calculator for simple math probleMs.

501
00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:34.710
So, did I know you when you made this commitment?

502
00:22:34.930 --> 00:22:36.310
Because I knew you in your 30s.

503
00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:36.870
Yeah.

504
00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:37.930
I guess you did.

505
00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:42.210
I guess I never added anything or subtracted anything around you.

506
00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:48.150
My best friend in her 30s took a year off from calculators and didn't tell me.

507
00:22:48.410 --> 00:22:50.470
I do weird stuff like that sometimes.

508
00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:51.850
What is that about me?

509
00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:55.830
I think you like to make hurdles for yourself.

510
00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:56.850
What?

511
00:22:57.030 --> 00:22:57.870
This is horrible.

512
00:22:58.170 --> 00:23:00.250
I should speak to a therapist about this.

513
00:23:01.190 --> 00:23:02.430
What am I doing?

514
00:23:02.550 --> 00:23:03.830
Why am I making my life harder?

515
00:23:03.890 --> 00:23:04.290
You know what?

